---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Principle from '../components/Principle.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="RiiR.bio - Rewrite it in Rust, for bioinformatics" activePage="manifesto">
  <Hero />

  <main class="manifesto">
    <div class="preamble container reveal-stagger">
      <p class="reveal">
        Bioinformatics has a technical debt problem. Our most trusted tools were written
        years or decades ago, when datasets were orders of magnitude smaller. They work
        correctly. They are thoroughly validated. They are also slow, dependency-heavy,
        and expensive to run at modern sequencing scale.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        AI coding assistants have changed the equation. A domain expert can now rewrite
        established tools in compiled languages in days, not years. Fast code has become
        cheap. The scientific insight, careful validation, and community trust behind the
        original tools have not.
      </p>
      <p class="preamble-callout reveal">
        A wave of AI-assisted rewrites is coming.<br />
        The question is not <em>whether</em> it will happen, but whether it will happen <em>well</em>.
      </p>
      <p class="reveal">
        These are the principles we follow.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Section id="philosophy" number="I" title="Philosophy">
      <Principle
        number="1.1"
        title="Credit the original authors"
        description="Always, everywhere. In the README, the docs, the paper, and any downstream reports. The scientific insight behind these tools took years to develop. A dedicated credits page should list every reimplemented tool with its full citation."
      />
      <Principle
        number="1.2"
        title="Emulate exactly, then optimise"
        description="The first goal is bit-identical or numerically-identical output. This inherits the validation history of the original tool and lets users switch with zero risk to their scientific conclusions. Optimisations that change output should be opt-in and clearly documented."
      />
      <Principle
        number="1.3"
        title="Be transparent about AI"
        description="If AI coding assistants were used, say so. This is a strength, not a weakness. It demonstrates that correctness was verified through output comparison, not code review alone. Document the development process honestly."
      />
    </Section>

    <Section id="planning" number="II" title="Planning">
      <Principle
        number="2.1"
        title="Think big"
        description="Combine tools. Eliminate redundant I/O. Remove upstream prerequisites. A rewrite that just ports one tool to a faster language misses the real opportunity: rethinking how multiple tools share work."
      />
      <Principle
        number="2.2"
        title="Work small"
        description="Small iteration loops. One tool at a time. Step-by-step validation against the original output before moving on. The compound complexity of rewriting many things at once will bury you."
      />
      <Principle
        number="2.3"
        title="Engage upstream"
        description="Collaborate with the original developers rather than working in isolation. Co-authorship, upstream contributions, or at minimum a conversation before public release. Turn a potential competitor into a welcome complement."
      />
    </Section>

    <Section id="building" number="III" title="Building">
      <Principle
        number="3.1"
        title="Test relentlessly"
        description="Different data types, different sizes, different edge cases. Missing prerequisites should give clear error messages. Don't settle for 'it works on my machine' - test on data that will break things."
      />
      <Principle
        number="3.2"
        title="Use real test data"
        description="Don't rely on artificial inputs. Real sequencing data has quirks, edge cases, and format variations that synthetic data never captures. Your tests should use the same data that production pipelines see."
      />
      <Principle
        number="3.3"
        title="Replicate, don't improve"
        description="Always target 1:1 replication of results. Avoid the temptation to 'fix' things along the way. Changing results voids trust and creates a maintenance burden. If you find genuine improvements, take them upstream first."
      />
      <Principle
        number="3.4"
        title="Pin versions and document"
        description="Pin the exact version of every original tool you're emulating. Document the comparison methodology. Future you (and every user) needs to know exactly what was validated against what."
      />
    </Section>

    <Section id="stewardship" number="IV" title="Stewardship">
      <Principle
        number="4.1"
        title="Maintain and govern"
        description="Adopt clear contribution guidelines, a code of conduct, and a release process. Respond to issues. An unmaintained rewrite is worse than no rewrite because it creates a false promise of continued support."
      />
      <Principle
        number="4.2"
        title="Preserve compatibility"
        description="Output formats should be identical to the originals. Downstream tools, pipeline integrations, and reporting frameworks should continue to work unmodified. This includes MultiQC modules, file naming conventions, and header formats."
      />
      <Principle
        number="4.3"
        title="Respect upstream licenses"
        description="Check the license of every tool you reimplement. Understand what it permits. Your rewrite's license should be at least as permissive as the most restrictive original."
      />
      <Principle
        number="4.4"
        title="Release as open source"
        description="The tools you're rewriting were built by the open-source community for the open-source community. Your rewrites should be too. Permissive licenses lower the barrier to adoption and contribution."
      />
      <Principle
        number="4.5"
        title="Don't file reckless bug reports"
        description="Before reporting a bug in the original tool, be sure. Really sure. Complete replication with the original tool, full understanding by a human. No automated AI bug reports - they put strain on volunteer maintainers."
      />
    </Section>

    <section class="closing container">
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="closing-line"></div>
        <p class="closing-text">
          The wave is coming.<br />
          Let us make sure it lifts all boats.
        </p>
        <p class="closing-attribution">
          {/* Read the paper: <em>The Bioinformatics Rewrite</em> (preprint coming soon)<br /> */}
          Plain text version: <a href="/manifesto.md">riir.bio/manifesto.md</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  .manifesto {
    padding-top: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .preamble {
    padding-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .preamble p {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
    max-width: 65ch;
  }

  .preamble-callout {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 1.05rem !important;
    color: var(--rust-400) !important;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--rust-600);
    padding-left: var(--space-lg);
    margin: var(--space-xl) 0 !important;
    line-height: 1.8 !important;
  }

  .preamble-callout em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--rust-300);
  }

  .closing {
    padding: var(--space-2xl) 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .closing-line {
    width: 48px;
    height: 2px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--rust-600), var(--rust-400));
    margin: 0 auto var(--space-xl);
  }

  .closing-text {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: clamp(1.2rem, 2.5vw, 1.6rem);
    color: var(--text-primary);
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xl);
  }

  .closing-text a {
    color: inherit;
  }
  .closing-text a:hover {
    color: var(--rust-400);
  }

  .closing-attribution {
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .closing-attribution a {
    color: inherit;
  }
  .closing-attribution a:hover {
    color: var(--rust-400);
  }

</style>
